The standard approach in .NET is to **wrap file system calls behind an interface** and inject it:

```csharp
public interface IFileSystem
{
    string ReadAllText(string path);
    void WriteAllText(string path, string contents);
    bool DirectoryExists(string path);
}
```

Then inject `IFileSystem` into your classes via constructor injection. In production, provide a thin implementation that delegates to `System.IO.File`/`Directory`. In tests, use a mock or an in-memory fake.

**Alternatively**, use the built-in `System.IO.Abstractions` NuGet package — it provides `IFileSystem` out of the box along with a `MockFileSystem` for testing, so you don't have to maintain your own wrapper.

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers  # for tests
```

**Quick example:**

```csharp
// Production code
public class ConfigLoader(IFileSystem fs)
{
    public string Load(string path) => fs.File.ReadAllText(path);
}

// Test
var fs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/config.json", new MockFileData("{}") }
});
var loader = new ConfigLoader(fs);
Assert.Equal("{}", loader.Load("/config.json"));
```

This keeps your logic unit-testable without touching the real disk.
